No — plain text/AST search alone is not reliable for this. Here's the concrete case in `core/RMS.UnifiedStore.Core/Clients/ServiceManagement/`:

**The pattern:** `IAuthApi` has two implementations — `AuthServiceClient` (real, calls SML over HTTP) and `LocalAuthServiceClient` (stub, reads a local JSON config). Every consumer (handlers, controllers, jobs — dozens of files) only ever references the interface `IAuthApi`, never either concrete class by name. The concrete type is picked at runtime in `service-api/RMS.UnifiedStore.Service.Api/App/ServiceFactory.cs:264-271`:

```csharp
if (_webHostEnvironment != null && _webHostEnvironment.IsDevelopment() && string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_baseUriAuthSvc))
{
    services.AddSingleton<IAuthApi>(sp => new LocalAuthServiceClient(sp.GetService<IConfigurationProvider>()));
}
else
{
    services.AddSingleton<IAuthApi, AuthServiceClient>();
}
```

Consequences for a text/AST search:

- Grepping for `LocalAuthServiceClient` finds its one DI registration and nothing else — it looks dead/unreferenced even though it's live in dev environments. A caller-tracing search rooted at `AuthServiceClient` (e.g. "who calls `GetTenantUsers` on the real client?") will miss every dev-environment code path, because those actually run against `LocalAuthServiceClient`.
- Grepping for calls to `IAuthApi.GetTenantGroupsAsync` finds all *interface* call sites correctly (there are dozens across `catalog-service`, `service-api`), but a search cannot tell you *which* concrete implementation executes for a given call site — that's decided by an environment check evaluated at process startup, not visible in the call graph at all.
- There's also `catalog-service/.../ModelBinding/OnBehalfOfModelBinder.cs:46`, which does `bindingContext.HttpContext.RequestServices.GetService<IAuthApi>()` — a runtime service-locator resolution. Static analysis sees a call to `IServiceProvider.GetService<T>()`, not a call to `AuthServiceClient` or `LocalAuthServiceClient` at all, so this caller won't show up in a caller search for either concrete class.

So: text/AST search finds all callers of the *interface*, which is usually good enough for "who calls this method" — but it cannot resolve which *concrete implementation* actually runs, and it will completely miss registrations that route through `IServiceProvider.GetService<T>()`, factory lambdas, or environment/config-conditional `AddSingleton<TInterface, TImpl>()` branches like the one above. Anyone asking "what calls `LocalAuthServiceClient`" via grep will get a false negative — the true answer is "any dev-environment request path through `IAuthApi`," which only a runtime trace or reading `ServiceFactory.cs` reveals.
